{%- if kind == "loopUnwinding" -%}
A loop needed more iterations than the bound unrolls. Constrain what determines the trip count, or raise the bound. A
summary directive cannot name a loop. If the property is about the loop, skip it and say what bound it needed.
{%- elif kind == "cannotOverflow" -%}
`Cannot overflow` is a check the Prover adds on its own, not one your rule asserts: it could not show that an expression
it treats as a pointer stays below 2^64. That is almost always because a value the rule reads from an account (a
balance, an amount) is free to sit just below u64::MAX. Bound the account-data values on that rule's path —
`cvlr_assume!(x < 1 << 63)` is enough — and record each bound as an assumption. A property about the top of the range
cannot take the bound: skip it and say so.
{%- endif -%}
